# deploy/collaborator — the whole configuration of a resolver and a delegate
# sharing one box.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# It is the union of `../resolver/.env.example` and `../delegate/.env.example`,
# with nothing added and nothing renamed: the same variables mean the same
# things, so moving to or from the separate directories is a copy, not a
# rewrite. Three lines matter, and they are all about your node — the delegate
# half needs no configuration at all, only its labels file.
#
# There is no §3 value here and none anywhere else: LAUNCH_HEIGHT, the four
# role addresses, RESERVED_NAMES and the O listing fee are constants in
# @nns/core since the launch freeze. That is deliberate — a value an operator
# can set is a value two resolvers can disagree about, and disagreeing
# resolvers is the one failure this whole design exists to prevent.

# ═══ The resolver half ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Your node (required) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A Nimiq history node whose retention covers LAUNCH_HEIGHT (58,842,720).
# README.md is explicit about what happens if it does not — read it before you
# reach for a workaround.
#
# `host.docker.internal` is the Docker host, so this default is "a node on the
# same machine, port 6488". Any reachable URL works; `127.0.0.1` will not,
# since inside a container that is the container.
NNS_RPC_URL=http://host.docker.internal:6488

# From the [rpc-server] section of the node's client.toml. Both blank if it
# has none — blank means absent, not an empty credential.
NNS_RPC_USER=
NNS_RPC_PASSWORD=

# ── Where the API is published (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Host bind for the API's port. Loopback, because what should be reachable
# from outside is your TLS terminator: the clients are browsers.
#NNS_API_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8635

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The container's own Postgres password. It never leaves the compose network —
# there is no published port — but a real one costs nothing.
#NNS_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=nns

# 24 is main-albatross.
#NNS_NETWORK_ID=24

# Tail poll interval, RPC timeout, retry count, heartbeat ceiling.
# ── Seeding an empty database (optional; default scratch) ────────────────────
# scratch replays every batch from LAUNCH_HEIGHT — hours, and the only mode
# that derives everything from the chain. snapshot downloads another
# operator's §8.2 log, verifies it against the §8.1 commitment they publish
# and scans from there — minutes, but blind to a message that log omitted.
# hybrid does both: usable at once, re-derived from the chain in the
# background. Read only when the database is empty. /params discloses the
# range under `verification`. packages/indexer/.env.example has the detail.
#NNS_START_MODE=scratch
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_URL=https://resolver.example.com
# With NNS_SNAPSHOT_SOURCE=anchor the expected commitment comes off the §9
# contract (quorum of listed publishers, two RPC endpoints) instead of from the
# peer serving the log, and NNS_SNAPSHOT_URL becomes an IPFS gateway root.
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_SOURCE=peer
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_ANCHOR_RPC=https://rpc-a.example.com,https://rpc-b.example.com
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_ANCHOR_CONTRACT=
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_ANCHOR_PUBLISHERS=
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_ANCHOR_QUORUM=2
# 250,000 (the reader default) is above the eth_getLogs cap of common free
# endpoints; publicnode's Sepolia caps at 50,000.
#NNS_SNAPSHOT_ANCHOR_LOOKBACK=45000

#NNS_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=15000
#NNS_RPC_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#NNS_RPC_ATTEMPTS=4
#NNS_PROGRESS_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# ═══ The delegate half ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Nothing here is required. The delegate serves correctly on 127.0.0.1:8636
# with every line below commented out — what it needs is the labels file, not
# a variable.

# ── The labels directory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host directory holding `labels.json`, mounted read-only at /labels. The
# directory rather than the file, because a single-file bind mount pins the
# inode and an editor's atomic save would leave the container serving stale
# bytes with a green healthcheck. Edits — in place or by rename — are picked
# up without a restart; a file that fails to parse leaves the previous one
# serving.
#NNS_DELEGATE_LABELS_DIR=./labels

# ── Where it listens ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host bind for the published port. Loopback, for the same terminator the API
# sits behind — on its own hostname, since §8.6's `D` is a bare host.
#NNS_DELEGATE_PUBLISH=127.0.0.1:8636

# Comma-separated names each cross-checked for presence in the labels file at
# boot, and used for nothing else — a miss is a misdeployed file, which is
# worth catching before it answers with another owner's addresses.
#NNS_DELEGATE_NAME=alice,bob

# Used for labels whose entry states no ttl. Clients cap caching at one hour
# regardless (§8.6 step 4).
#NNS_DELEGATE_DEFAULT_TTL=300

# How often the labels file's mtime and size are checked. SIGHUP forces it.
#NNS_DELEGATE_RELOAD_SEC=5

# ═══ Both halves ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# One level for all four containers — they share this `.env`, so this is not
# per-service. debug prints a line per batch and per candidate in the indexer:
# useful while you watch the first backfill, far too much left on.
#NNS_LOG_LEVEL=info
